  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"111\" data-end=\"275\"><strong data-start=\"111\" data-end=\"142\">C4 (Complement Component 4)</strong> is part of the <strong data-start=\"158\" data-end=\"190\">classical complement pathway</strong>, a crucial arm of the immune system that helps clear pathogens and immune complexes.</p>\n<p data-start=\"277\" data-end=\"300\">The C4 test is used to:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"302\" data-end=\"671\">\n<li data-start=\"302\" data-end=\"389\">\n<p data-start=\"304\" data-end=\"389\">Evaluate <strong data-start=\"313\" data-end=\"336\">autoimmune diseases</strong>, particularly <strong data-start=\"351\" data-end=\"389\">Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE)</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"390\" data-end=\"512\">\n<p data-start=\"392\" data-end=\"512\">Help diagnose and monitor <strong data-start=\"418\" data-end=\"449\">hereditary angioedema (HAE)</strong> (used alongside <strong data-start=\"466\" data-end=\"491\">C1 esterase inhibitor</strong> and <strong data-start=\"496\" data-end=\"503\">C1q</strong> testing)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"513\" data-end=\"600\">\n<p data-start=\"515\" data-end=\"600\">Assess <strong data-start=\"522\" data-end=\"550\">immune complex disorders</strong>, such as <strong data-start=\"560\" data-end=\"574\">vasculitis</strong> or <strong data-start=\"578\" data-end=\"600\">glomerulonephritis</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"601\" data-end=\"671\">\n<p data-start=\"603\" data-end=\"671\">Investigate <strong data-start=\"615\" data-end=\"643\">unexplained inflammation</strong> or <strong data-start=\"647\" data-end=\"671\">recurrent infections</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong data-start=\"647\" data-end=\"671\">Decreased C4 &ndash; Clinical Implications</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Condition</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Clinical Significance</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE)</strong></td>\n<td>Low C4 due to complement consumption from immune complex activation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Hereditary Angioedema (HAE)</strong></td>\n<td>Typically low during and between attacks; helps differentiate <strong>Type I/II HAE</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Glomerulonephritis (e.g., lupus nephritis)</strong></td>\n<td>C4 may be low due to ongoing immune activation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Cryoglobulinemia</strong></td>\n<td>Often shows reduced C4 due to classical complement activation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Chronic infections or inflammation</strong></td>\n<td>Can cause mild reduction in C4 due to immune system engagement</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Congenital C4 deficiency</strong></td>\n<td>Rare; increases risk for autoimmune conditions and recurrent infections</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>When interpreting low C4, it is essential to co-assess C3. A low C3 with low C4 suggests classical pathway activation (e.g., SLE), while low C3 with normal C4 points toward alternative pathway activation (e.g., post-infectious GN).</p>",
